; SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
;
; TypeDump — our half of the CHECKER yardstick's dump.
;
; ★★★ THIS IS NOT A PORT OF ANYTHING, in the same sense SymbolDump is not: the
; format is OURS, invented with tests/oracle/types.go and described in
; TESTPLAN.md's *The fifth yardstick*, and this file is its second producer. Two
; sections, in this order:
;
;   C <pos> <end> <code>                 one line per checker diagnostic
;   T <kind> <pos> <end> <typeString>    one line per node the type walk asks about
;
; It lives inside the package rather than in the dumper PROGRAM for SymbolDump's
; reason: it walks the tree and holds a builder, and a generic instance handed to a
; program root reads back as garbage (root CLAUDE.md). The program asks for the
; whole dump in one call and prints nothing itself.
;
; ── The WALK is transcribed, and that is a contract rather than a convenience ─
;
; ★★★ `forEachASTNode` and `skipForType` are the reference's own baseline walker
; (testutil/tsbaseline/type_symbol_baseline.go), and the oracle transcribes them
; for a reason that applies to this side identically: GetTypeAtLocation at EVERY
; node nil-dereferences inside the reference, so the exclusions are what keep the
; oracle alive — *"don't try to get the type of something that is already a type"*,
; with the type-alias name as its exception. And on THIS side the same list is what
; decides which nodes appear in the T section: a port whose predicate differed
; would show up as a shifted list of positions with no clue which test moved. So
; the two halves of the yardstick transcribe the same walker, and each says so.
;
; ★ The one exclusion that is NOT the reference's is the nameless type-only import
; clause, and it is the oracle's own guard against a crash inside the checker
; (`import type { A } from './x'` is a nil dereference through a public API). It is
; mirrored here because a node the oracle cannot ask about must not appear on this
; side either — the exclusion is part of the FORMAT, not of the reference.
;
; ★★ The walk is RECURSIVE here where the reference uses an explicit stack with a
; reversal. That is the same order — the stack version pushes the children reversed
; precisely so that popping yields pre-order — and the Reparsed filtering is
; preserved exactly, including the property that a node the outer condition rejects
; has its CHILDREN skipped too (the reference's `if` wraps both the append and the
; child push). Recursion is this port's shape for a walk because Scaly's only
; escaping-closure form is `return \x: …`, so a fresh visitor closure per level is
; what the escape checker exists to reject (see ast.scaly and tscaly_ast.scaly).
;
; ★ `elem.Parent` is asked of the NODE rather than threaded through the walk,
; because the reference asks it of the node and because slice 26 measured that the
; two are the same thing: over 1 467 387 nodes, a node's Parent is exactly its
; parent in the ForEachChild walk. The source file, which has none, is the only
; node whose parent is read at all — and it is not, because both conditions
; short-circuit on `Reparsed == 0` first, which the reference relies on for the
; same reason (a nil deref otherwise).
;
; ── What suppresses the dump ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; Either half may meet something this slice does not implement, and then NOTHING
; is printed: the checker's report is the unit's whole answer. That is the
; parser's and the binder's arrangement, and the reason is theirs — a partial dump
; would be claiming agreement it has not earned. So `write` answers false and the
; program root prints the UNPORTED line from the checker's own fields.

use scaly.memory.Page
use scaly.containers.String
use scaly.containers.StringBuilder
use scaly.io.Console
use tscaly.ast.AstNode
use tscaly.checker.Checker
use tscaly.checker.Type

define TypeDump
(
    host: ref[Page]
    out: ref[StringBuilder]
    c: ref[Checker]
    file: ref[AstNode]?

    ; ── the walk-only mode, and why the instrument exists ───────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ WITHOUT IT THE WALK IS UNMEASURED, AND THAT IS THE CHECKER DIMENSION'S
    ; OWN COUPLING SEEN FROM THE CONTROL SIDE. The C section gates the T section
    ; (checker.scaly's header has the measurement), so a unit whose statements are
    ; unported never reaches the walk at all — and in slice 47 that is 1 030 of
    ; 1 038 units. So everything transcribed here beyond the end-of-file token —
    ; forEachASTNode's two Reparsed conditions, skipForType's four exclusions,
    ; ast.IsPartOfTypeNode's twenty arms, GetMeaningFromDeclaration's six, the
    ; nameless-import guard — would be code no control could turn red, which is
    ; exactly what PathCheck and NumCheck exist to prevent one dimension down: *an
    ; unexercised arm is indistinguishable from a correct one*.
    ;
    ; In this mode the walk prints the NODE LIST it would ask about and asks for no
    ; types, so it cannot report and runs on every unit of the corpus:
    ;
    ;   W <kind> <pos> <end>
    ;
    ; and tests/walkcheck.sh diffs that against the `T <kind> <pos> <end>` prefix
    ; of the reference's own dump, unit for unit. ★ What it deliberately does NOT
    ; check is the TYPE — the third field of a T line is not in a W line at all —
    ; because that is the yardstick's job and an instrument that pretended to
    ; answer it would be worse than none (tests/checktypes.py's own boundary, from
    ; the other side).
    walk_only: bool
)
{
    ; The entry point the dumper program calls: a checked file in, one dump out,
    ; written to stdout in one write. Answers false when the check reported
    ; something this slice does not implement, in which case nothing is printed.
    function write(host: ref[Page], c: ref[Checker], file: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
        TypeDump.emit(host, c, file, false, false, false)

    ; The walk instrument's entry point: the same walk, printing the node list.
    ; Always answers true — nothing in this mode can report.
    function write_walk(host: ref[Page], c: ref[Checker], file: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
        TypeDump.emit(host, c, file, true, false, false)

    ; ★★★ THE SECOND INSTRUMENT'S ENTRY POINT (slice 48), and the argument for it
    ; is walkcheck's turned around. That one exists because the C section GATES the
    ; T section, so nothing of the WALK is measured; this one exists because the
    ; same gate hides the C section's own arms — a unit whose check stops at its
    ; first statement produces a dump nobody compares, so the grammar checks, the
    ; span grammarErrorOnFirstToken computes and the SORT would all be code no
    ; control could reach in 1 031 of 1 040 units.
    ;
    ; So this mode prints the C section AND NOTHING ELSE, and prints it whether or
    ; not the check reported. What comes out is therefore a PREFIX of the check —
    ; the diagnostics found before it stopped — which is not the yardstick's
    ; question and is a sound one of its own: tests/diagcheck.sh asserts our lines
    ; are a SUBSEQUENCE of the reference's. See that script for what the relation
    ; can and cannot catch.
    function write_diags(host: ref[Page], c: ref[Checker], file: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
        TypeDump.emit(host, c, file, false, true, false)

    ; ★★★ THE THIRD INSTRUMENT'S ENTRY POINT (slice 75): EVERY stop of the unit, in
    ; the order they happened, one `S <tag> <detail>` line each.
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHY IT IS NOT THE WORK LIST. `record_unported` is FIRST-WINS while the
    ; walk continues, so the histogram triage.py reads is a histogram of FIRSTS —
    ; and a stop that many units reach reads as ZERO there whenever they all stop
    ; somewhere else first. Measured on the stage-1 corpus the day this landed:
    ; `class-extends-heritage-clause` is 0 in the work list and 27 units / 92 events
    ; here. **A row of zero and a row nothing reaches are the same row in that
    ; instrument, and they are different facts.**
    ;
    ; ★★ IT PRINTS WHETHER OR NOT THE CHECK REPORTED, like write_diags and for the
    ; same reason turned one notch: a stop is exactly what this mode measures, so
    ; returning early on one would print nothing precisely when there is something
    ; to say. It always answers true.
    ;
    ; ★ THE DETAIL COLUMN IS KEPT because the tag alone conflates arms — the work
    ; list already distinguishes `check-index-constraints (detail 264)` from
    ; `(detail 265)`, which is the class arm from the interface one, and an
    ; aggregation that dropped it would merge two chapters into one row.
    ;
    ; ★ `file` is unread and kept, so the dumper calls all three instruments with
    ; one signature; the log is a property of the CHECKER, not of the tree.
    function write_stops(host: ref[Page], c: ref[Checker], file: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        ; ★★★ THE WALK RUNS HERE, AND SLICE 75 LEFT IT OUT — WHICH IS WHY THE GATE
        ; WAS SILENT ABOUT 32 UNITS AND THE LOOP CALLED THEM SOMETHING THEY ARE NOT.
        ; The dumper's stop mode returned right after `get_diagnostics()`, so a unit
        ; whose CHECK completes and whose DUMP stops logged nothing at all while
        ; `types.ours` carried an UNPORTED line — and checkloop.py's stops arm files
        ; that combination under `other`, explained as *"the checker never ran, so
        ; the log is legitimately empty"*. Measured on the stage-1 corpus: **all 32
        ; of them read `UNPORTED 0 type-of-node <kind>`, a tag only this walk can
        ; raise.** Not one was a parse or a bind stop. ★★The label was wrong on the
        ; day it was written and nothing could see it, because the bucket was
        ; ARGUED rather than sampled; slice 76 found it by moving four more units
        ; into it. **A bucket a gate does not enter needs its contents PRINTED, not
        ; a sentence about them.**
        ;
        ; ★★★ AND WHAT IT BUYS IS THE MORE USEFUL NUMBER: those 32 are the units
        ; whose CHECK IS COMPLETE and whose only remaining wall is one arm of
        ; `getTypeOfNode`. They are the closest thing this port has to a frontier,
        ; and they were invisible in every histogram.
        ;
        ; ★ The dump's own output is discarded (emit's `stops_only`), so this mode
        ; still prints nothing but `S` lines. The checker is the SAME one
        ; get_diagnostics ran on, which is what the dumper's `--sections` mode does
        ; for its own dump — so the log this prints is the log the recorded UNPORTED
        ; line came from, and the gate can compare them.
        TypeDump.emit(host, c, file, false, false, true)
        let sb StringBuilder^host()
        let n Checker.stop_count(c)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            let tag Checker.stop_tag_at(c, i)
            sb.append "S "
            ; ★ `sb.append String(tag)` is THREE operands — the member-access head,
            ; the Type and the tuple — so the parenless call swallows `String` and
            ; the arity gate reports `got 0, expected 1`. Hold the value in a local
            ; (root CLAUDE.md's parenless-chained-call trap).
            if tag = null
                sb.append "no-tag"
            else
            {
                let t String(tag)
                sb.append t
            }
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.stop_detail_at(c, i))
            sb.append("\n" as char)
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let s sb.to_string()
        Console.print(s.to_c_string())
        true
    }

    ; write_flow — the FLOWPIN (slice 93). Its argument is in FlowTraceEvent's
    ; header: this chapter's product is a flow TYPE, and no instrument in this
    ; directory can see one, because the T section is emitted only for a unit whose
    ; check COMPLETES and no unit that reaches the flow does. It runs the walk for
    ; write_stops' reason.
    ;
    ; ★ `F <pos> <end>` per invocation, `V <flags>` per visited flow node in the
    ; order the walk visits them, and `A <typeid> <stopped>` for the answer. The
    ; VISIT SEQUENCE is the column the battery moves: an arm that takes a different
    ; edge writes a different list even where the answer is unchanged, and an arm
    ; that answers a different type writes a different id even where the edges are.
    function write_flow(host: ref[Page], c: ref[Checker], file: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        TypeDump.emit(host, c, file, false, false, true)
        let sb StringBuilder^host()
        let n Checker.flow_trace_count(c)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            let kind Checker.flow_trace_field_at(c, i, 0)
            if kind = 0
            {
                sb.append "F "
                sb.append(Checker.flow_trace_field_at(c, i, 1))
                sb.append(" " as char)
                sb.append(Checker.flow_trace_field_at(c, i, 2))
            }
            if kind = 1
            {
                sb.append "V "
                sb.append(Checker.flow_trace_field_at(c, i, 1))
            }
            if kind = 2
            {
                sb.append "A "
                sb.append(Checker.flow_trace_field_at(c, i, 1))
                sb.append(" " as char)
                sb.append(Checker.flow_trace_field_at(c, i, 2))
            }
            sb.append("\n" as char)
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let s sb.to_string()
        Console.print(s.to_c_string())
        true
    }

    ; write_unions — the UNION PIN (slice 94). Its argument is in
    ; Checker.union_type_count's header: the FLOWPIN sees a union's ID and the stop
    ; log sees where a walk stopped, and neither can see the two halves this chapter
    ; is mostly made of — the constituent ORDER CompareTypes decides and the NAME
    ; formatUnionTypes and type_to_string compose out of it. It runs the walk for
    ; write_stops' reason.
    ;
    ; ★ `U <id> <flags> <objectflags> <name>` per interned union, in creation order.
    ; The NAME is what the printer would put in a T line; when the printer cannot
    ; name it — a constituent whose own arm reports — the column is `?`, which is a
    ; value the pin can compare rather than a line it drops.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE FLAGS COLUMN IS NOT DECORATION: booleanType is the union that carries
    ; TypeFlagsBoolean, and that one bit is the whole reason `boolean` prints as a
    ; keyword instead of as `false | true`. A control that drops it moves this
    ; column and the name column together, which is what tells a reader the two are
    ; one mechanism.
    function write_unions(host: ref[Page], c: ref[Checker], file: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        TypeDump.emit(host, c, file, false, false, true)
        let sb StringBuilder^host()
        let n Checker.union_type_count(c)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            let e Checker.union_type_at(c, i)
            if e <> null
            {
                let t e as ref[Type]
                sb.append "U "
                sb.append(t.id)
                sb.append(" " as char)
                sb.append(t.flags)
                sb.append(" " as char)
                sb.append(t.object_flags)
                sb.append(" " as char)
                let nb StringBuilder^host()
                if c.type_to_string(t, nb)
                {
                    let nm nb.to_string()
                    sb.append(nm)
                }
                else
                    sb.append("?" as char)
                sb.append("\n" as char)
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let s sb.to_string()
        Console.print(s.to_c_string())
        true
    }

    ; write_typerefs — the TYPEREF PIN (slice 95). Its argument is in
    ; TypeRefEvent's header: the T section is all-or-nothing per unit, so the
    ; chapter's product is invisible on every unit that still has any other wall —
    ; 1 466 of 1 480 at stage 1. It runs the walk for write_stops' reason.
    ;
    ; ★ `X <pos> <end> <symbolflags> <typeid> <name>` per RESOLVED type reference,
    ; in the order the check reached them. The NAME column is what the printer
    ; would put in a T line, and `?` where the printer cannot make one — which is a
    ; value a control can move rather than a line it drops.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE SYMBOLFLAGS COLUMN IS THE OTHER HALF AND IT IS NOT DECORATION: the two
    ; things this chapter can get wrong are WHICH SYMBOL a name resolves to and
    ; WHAT the type is then called, and a wrong symbol with a right-looking name is
    ; exactly the shape a name-only pin cannot see. A control that redirects the
    ; resolution moves this column at an unchanged name whenever the two symbols
    ; share a spelling.
    ; write_props — the PROPPIN (slice 96). Its argument is in PropEvent's header,
    ; and it is slice 95's argument one product on: the chapter's product is an
    ; ANSWER GIVEN AT A NODE and the T section is all-or-nothing per unit, so
    ; nothing else in this directory can see a property access that the check
    ; resolved. It runs the walk for write_stops' reason.
    ;
    ; ★ `P <pos> <end> <symbolflags> <apparentid> <typeid> <name>` per RESOLVED
    ; property access, in the order the check reached them. The three middle
    ; columns are the three halves that can go wrong separately — which member, on
    ; what receiver, answering what — and `?` marks a type the printer cannot name,
    ; which is a value a control can MOVE rather than a line it drops.
    function write_props(host: ref[Page], c: ref[Checker], file: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        TypeDump.emit(host, c, file, false, false, true)
        let sb StringBuilder^host()
        let n Checker.prop_count(c)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            sb.append "P "
            sb.append(Checker.prop_field_at(c, i, 0))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.prop_field_at(c, i, 1))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.prop_field_at(c, i, 2))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.prop_field_at(c, i, 3))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.prop_field_at(c, i, 4))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            let t Checker.prop_type_at(c, i)
            let nb StringBuilder^host()
            if c.type_to_string(t, nb)
            {
                let nm nb.to_string()
                sb.append(nm)
            }
            else
                sb.append("?" as char)
            sb.append("\n" as char)
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let s sb.to_string()
        Console.print(s.to_c_string())
        true
    }

    ; write_this — the THISPIN (slice 97). Its argument is in ThisEvent's header:
    ; the chapter's product is the type a `this` answers in the container the
    ; normalisation loop settled on, and no other artifact in this directory prints
    ; either half. It runs the walk for write_stops' reason.
    ;
    ; ★ `H <pos> <end> <containerkind> <arm> <typeid> <name>` per `this` expression
    ; the chapter ANSWERED, in the order the check reached them. `?` marks a type
    ; the printer cannot name, which is a value a control can MOVE rather than a
    ; line it drops.
    function write_this(host: ref[Page], c: ref[Checker], file: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        TypeDump.emit(host, c, file, false, false, true)
        let sb StringBuilder^host()
        let n Checker.this_count(c)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            sb.append "H "
            sb.append(Checker.this_field_at(c, i, 0))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.this_field_at(c, i, 1))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.this_field_at(c, i, 2))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.this_field_at(c, i, 3))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.this_field_at(c, i, 4))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            let t Checker.this_type_at(c, i)
            let nb StringBuilder^host()
            if c.type_to_string(t, nb)
            {
                let nm nb.to_string()
                sb.append(nm)
            }
            else
                sb.append("?" as char)
            sb.append("\n" as char)
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let s sb.to_string()
        Console.print(s.to_c_string())
        true
    }

    ; write_with_this — the WITHPIN (slice 98). Its argument is in WithThisEvent's
    ; header, and it is the sharpest case yet of the reason the last four
    ; instruments exist: this chapter's answer has the SAME PRINTED NAME as its
    ; input, so even a unit whose T section were complete would not show it. It runs
    ; the walk for write_stops' reason.
    ;
    ; ★ `W <inid> <argid> <outid> <arm> <name>` per call getTypeWithThisArgument
    ; ANSWERED, in the order the check reached them. `argid` is 0 where the arm
    ; appended nothing, and `?` marks a type the printer cannot name — a value a
    ; control can MOVE rather than a line it drops.
    function write_with_this(host: ref[Page], c: ref[Checker], file: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        TypeDump.emit(host, c, file, false, false, true)
        let sb StringBuilder^host()
        let n Checker.with_count(c)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            sb.append "W "
            sb.append(Checker.with_field_at(c, i, 0))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.with_field_at(c, i, 1))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.with_field_at(c, i, 2))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.with_field_at(c, i, 3))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            let t Checker.with_type_at(c, i)
            let nb StringBuilder^host()
            if c.type_to_string(t, nb)
            {
                let nm nb.to_string()
                sb.append(nm)
            }
            else
                sb.append("?" as char)
            sb.append("\n" as char)
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let s sb.to_string()
        Console.print(s.to_c_string())
        true
    }

    ; write_objlits — the OBJPIN (slice 99). Its argument is in ObjLitEvent's
    ; header: an object literal's type is ANONYMOUS, so `type_to_string` renders its
    ; MEMBERS and three of the four things this chapter can get wrong — the property
    ; symbol's flags, the type's object flags and its IDENTITY — do not move that
    ; string. It runs the walk for write_stops' reason.
    ;
    ; ★ `O <pos> <typeid> <objectflags> <propcount> <arm> <name>` per object literal
    ; TYPE this chapter built, in the order the check reached them. `?` marks a type
    ; the printer cannot name — a value a control can MOVE rather than a line it
    ; drops.
    function write_objlits(host: ref[Page], c: ref[Checker], file: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        TypeDump.emit(host, c, file, false, false, true)
        let sb StringBuilder^host()
        let n Checker.objlit_count(c)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            sb.append "O "
            sb.append(Checker.objlit_field_at(c, i, 0))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.objlit_field_at(c, i, 1))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.objlit_field_at(c, i, 2))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.objlit_field_at(c, i, 3))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.objlit_field_at(c, i, 4))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            let t Checker.objlit_type_at(c, i)
            let nb StringBuilder^host()
            if c.type_to_string(t, nb)
            {
                let nm nb.to_string()
                sb.append(nm)
            }
            else
                sb.append("?" as char)
            sb.append("\n" as char)
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let s sb.to_string()
        Console.print(s.to_c_string())
        true
    }

    ; write_functions — the FNPIN (slice 100). Its argument is in FnEvent's
    ; header: this chapter's decision is three bits no other instrument prints,
    ; and its type is the SYMBOL's, which `--members` already covers. It runs the
    ; walk for write_stops' reason.
    ;
    ; ★ `F <pos> <kind> <sensitive> <ctxsig> <arm> <typeid>` per function
    ; expression, arrow function or object-literal method this chapter ANSWERED, in
    ; the order the check reached them. There is no NAME column and that is
    ; deliberate: the type is anonymous, so a name column would be `?` on every row
    ; the way the OBJPIN's is, and this pin has a cheaper thing to spend a column
    ; on — the kind, which separates three populations that take different arms.
    function write_functions(host: ref[Page], c: ref[Checker], file: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        TypeDump.emit(host, c, file, false, false, true)
        let sb StringBuilder^host()
        let n Checker.fn_count(c)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            sb.append "F "
            sb.append(Checker.fn_field_at(c, i, 0))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.fn_field_at(c, i, 1))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.fn_field_at(c, i, 2))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.fn_field_at(c, i, 3))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.fn_field_at(c, i, 4))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.fn_field_at(c, i, 5))
            sb.append("\n" as char)
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let s sb.to_string()
        Console.print(s.to_c_string())
        true
    }

    ; write_relations — the RELPIN (slice 101). Its argument is in RelEvent's
    ; header: the relation's commonest product is an ABSENCE, so the one thing no
    ; other instrument can see is that a comparison was made at all and what it
    ; concluded. It runs the walk for write_stops' reason.
    ;
    ; ★ `A <pos> <source flags> <target flags> <verdict>` per call of
    ; checkTypeRelatedToAndOptionallyElaborate, in the order the check made them.
    ; The two flag words stand in for a name column: the arms of
    ; isSimpleTypeRelatedTo are written over flags, so the flags are what separates
    ; one population of rows from another, and no printer is needed to read them.
    function write_relations(host: ref[Page], c: ref[Checker], file: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        TypeDump.emit(host, c, file, false, false, true)
        let sb StringBuilder^host()
        let n Checker.rel_count(c)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            sb.append "A "
            sb.append(Checker.rel_field_at(c, i, 0))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.rel_field_at(c, i, 1))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.rel_field_at(c, i, 2))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.rel_field_at(c, i, 3))
            sb.append("\n" as char)
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let s sb.to_string()
        Console.print(s.to_c_string())
        true
    }

    ; write_kinds — the KINDPIN (slice 102). Its argument is in KindEvent's header:
    ; the assignable-to-kind test answers most of its calls on a flag mask, and an
    ; answer there leaves no diagnostic, no type, no relation row and no stop. It
    ; runs the walk for write_stops' reason.
    ;
    ; ★ `K <source flags> <kind> <strict> <arm> <verdict>` per call of
    ; isTypeAssignableToKindEx, in the order the check made them. There is no
    ; position column and there cannot be: the function takes a type and a mask,
    ; not a node, so what anchors a row is its ORDER and its two words.
    function write_kinds(host: ref[Page], c: ref[Checker], file: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        TypeDump.emit(host, c, file, false, false, true)
        let sb StringBuilder^host()
        let n Checker.kind_count(c)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            sb.append "K "
            sb.append(Checker.kind_field_at(c, i, 0))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.kind_field_at(c, i, 1))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.kind_field_at(c, i, 2))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.kind_field_at(c, i, 3))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.kind_field_at(c, i, 4))
            sb.append("\n" as char)
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let s sb.to_string()
        Console.print(s.to_c_string())
        true
    }

    ; write_forks — the FORKPIN (slice 103). Its argument is in ForkEvent's header:
    ; this slice turns the structured branch's one stop into an ANSWER, and an
    ; answer there leaves no diagnostic, no type, no relation row and — once the
    ; stop is gone — nothing in the stop log either. It runs the walk for
    ; write_stops' reason.
    ;
    ; ★ `F <source flags> <target flags> <route> <branch> <verdict>` per call of
    ; the structured branch of isRelatedToEx, in the order the check made them.
    ; There is no position column and there cannot be, for write_kinds' reason:
    ; the branch takes two types and a state word, not a node.
    function write_forks(host: ref[Page], c: ref[Checker], file: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        TypeDump.emit(host, c, file, false, false, true)
        let sb StringBuilder^host()
        let n Checker.fork_count(c)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            sb.append "F "
            sb.append(Checker.fork_field_at(c, i, 0))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.fork_field_at(c, i, 1))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.fork_field_at(c, i, 2))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.fork_field_at(c, i, 3))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.fork_field_at(c, i, 4))
            sb.append("\n" as char)
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let s sb.to_string()
        Console.print(s.to_c_string())
        true
    }

    ; write_calls — the CALLPIN (slice 108). Its argument is in CallEvent's header:
    ; this slice's product is a SIGNATURE, and no other gate in this package can see
    ; one. It runs the walk for write_stops' reason.
    ;
    ; ★ `L <pos> <kind> <verdict> <argcount> <paramcount> <minargs>` per call of
    ; resolve_signature's two ported doors, in the order the check resolved them.
    function write_calls(host: ref[Page], c: ref[Checker], file: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        TypeDump.emit(host, c, file, false, false, true)
        let sb StringBuilder^host()
        let n Checker.call_count(c)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            sb.append "L "
            sb.append(Checker.call_field_at(c, i, 0))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.call_field_at(c, i, 1))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.call_field_at(c, i, 2))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.call_field_at(c, i, 3))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.call_field_at(c, i, 4))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.call_field_at(c, i, 5))
            sb.append("\n" as char)
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let s sb.to_string()
        Console.print(s.to_c_string())
        true
    }

    function write_typerefs(host: ref[Page], c: ref[Checker], file: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        TypeDump.emit(host, c, file, false, false, true)
        let sb StringBuilder^host()
        let n Checker.typeref_count(c)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            sb.append "X "
            sb.append(Checker.typeref_field_at(c, i, 0))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.typeref_field_at(c, i, 1))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.typeref_field_at(c, i, 2))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            sb.append(Checker.typeref_field_at(c, i, 3))
            sb.append(" " as char)
            let t Checker.typeref_type_at(c, i)
            let nb StringBuilder^host()
            if c.type_to_string(t, nb)
            {
                let nm nb.to_string()
                sb.append(nm)
            }
            else
                sb.append("?" as char)
            sb.append("\n" as char)
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let s sb.to_string()
        Console.print(s.to_c_string())
        true
    }

    ; write_members — the MEMBER PIN (slice 86). Its argument is in MemberEvent's
    ; header: the slice's product is a members table with one reader, so a control
    ; that breaks the mapper or the minting branch moves nothing any of the other
    ; five instruments can see. It runs the walk for write_stops' reason — a unit
    ; whose CHECK completes and whose DUMP does not would otherwise log a table
    ; the recorded answer does not match.
    ;
    ; ★ `R <objectflags> <props> <signatures> <indexes>` per resolved type, then
    ; `P <name> <symbolflags> <checkflags> <declarations>` per property and
    ; `G <flags> <typeparams> <params> <minargs>` per signature. The CHECK flags
    ; column is the one slice 86's battery is for: 1 means the member was MINTED.
    ; The G line is slice 87's — MemberEvent's header carries the measurement that
    ; forced it: three of that slice's fixtures have byte-identical member logs
    ; without it.
    function write_members(host: ref[Page], c: ref[Checker], file: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        TypeDump.emit(host, c, file, false, false, true)
        let sb StringBuilder^host()
        let n Checker.member_count(c)
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            let kind Checker.member_field_at(c, i, 0)
            if kind = 0
            {
                sb.append "R "
                sb.append(Checker.member_field_at(c, i, 1))
                sb.append(" " as char)
                sb.append(Checker.member_field_at(c, i, 2))
                sb.append(" " as char)
                sb.append(Checker.member_field_at(c, i, 3))
                sb.append(" " as char)
                sb.append(Checker.member_field_at(c, i, 4))
            }
            if kind = 2
            {
                sb.append "G "
                sb.append(Checker.member_field_at(c, i, 1))
                sb.append(" " as char)
                sb.append(Checker.member_field_at(c, i, 2))
                sb.append(" " as char)
                sb.append(Checker.member_field_at(c, i, 3))
                sb.append(" " as char)
                sb.append(Checker.member_field_at(c, i, 4))
            }
            if kind = 1
            {
                sb.append "P "
                let nd Checker.member_name_data_at(c, i)
                let nl Checker.member_field_at(c, i, 5)
                var j 0
                while j < nl
                {
                    let ch *(nd + j)
                    sb.append ch
                    set j: j + 1
                }
                sb.append(" " as char)
                sb.append(Checker.member_field_at(c, i, 1))
                sb.append(" " as char)
                sb.append(Checker.member_field_at(c, i, 2))
                sb.append(" " as char)
                sb.append(Checker.member_field_at(c, i, 3))
            }
            sb.append("\n" as char)
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let s sb.to_string()
        Console.print(s.to_c_string())
        true
    }

    function emit(host: ref[Page], c: ref[Checker], file: ref[AstNode], walk_only: bool, diags_only: bool, stops_only: bool) returns bool
    {
        let d host.allocate(sizeof TypeDump, alignof TypeDump) as pointer[TypeDump]
        set d.host: host
        set d.walk_only: walk_only
        ; `StringBuilder^host()` and NOT allocate-then-assign: its init allocates
        ; through Page.get(this), so building it as a temporary first writes a bump
        ; pointer over a page header somewhere in arbitrary memory and the crash
        ; lands at the NEXT allocation (root CLAUDE.md; SymbolDump's own note has
        ; the measurement).
        let sb StringBuilder^host()
        set d.out: sb
        set d.c: c
        set d.file: file

        ; ── C: the checker's diagnostics ───────────────────────────────────
        ;
        ; Read back through the collection's sorted view — sorted since slice 48,
        ; which is the slice that gave it something to sort (TS1046 and the two
        ; ambient-context statement diagnostics); DiagnosticList.sort_by_position
        ; carries the transcription of CompareDiagnostics and the argument for
        ; stopping at the code. ★ The WALK instrument has no C section at all: its
        ; caller does not run the check, so a count read here would be a count of
        ; nothing and the section would say something that instrument does not
        ; measure. The DIAGNOSTICS instrument is the opposite — it is nothing but
        ; this section.
        if walk_only = false
        {
            var i 0
            while i < Checker.diagnostic_count(c)
            {
                var pos 0
                var stop 0
                var code 0
                Checker.diagnostic_at(c, i, &pos, &stop, &code)
                d.out.append("C ")
                d.out.append(pos)
                d.out.append(" " as char)
                d.out.append(stop)
                d.out.append(" " as char)
                d.out.append(code)
                d.out.append("\n" as char)
                set i: i + 1
            }
        }

        ; ── T: the type of every node the walk may ask about ───────────────
        ;
        ; ★ The diagnostics instrument stops here, and the report does NOT suppress
        ; its output — that is the whole difference between it and the dump. A C
        ; section printed past a report is a claim about what was found, never a
        ; claim that nothing else would have been.
        if diags_only = false
        {
            d.walk(file)
            ; ★ Walk-only is exempt for the reason `walk`'s own guard is (see
            ; there): it computes no type, so the only report that can be standing
            ; is the CONSTRUCTOR's, and suppressing the node list for it prints
            ; nothing where the instrument's whole product is the list.
            if walk_only = false
            {
                if Checker.unported_at(c) >= 0
                    return false
            }
        }

        ; ★★★ THE STOP MODE RUNS THE WHOLE DUMP AND PRINTS NONE OF IT (slice 76).
        ; It is here rather than in a second walk driver because the thing it has to
        ; cover is exactly THIS walk — see write_stops' own note for what went wrong
        ; while it did not.
        if stops_only
            return true
        let s d.out.to_string()
        Console.print(s.to_c_string())
        true
    }

    ; forEachASTNode, transcribed. The two conditions are the reference's, in its
    ; order, and each is spelled out at its own lines because they are not the same
    ; condition: the OUTER one decides whether to descend, the INNER one whether to
    ; ask about this node — a reparsed node under an `as` or `satisfies` is asked
    ; about, and a reparsed node that merely CONTAINS one is descended into without
    ; being asked.
    procedure walk(this, elem: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        if elem = null
            return
        ; ★★★ THE WALK INSTRUMENT IS NOT GATED ON A REPORT, AND SLICE 113 IS WHERE
        ; THAT BECAME MEASURABLE. The guard is right for the DUMP — a T section
        ; printed past a stop would claim a type nobody computed — and wrong for
        ; `--walk`, which computes no type at all: the only stop that can be
        ; standing when it runs is one the CONSTRUCTOR recorded, and a walk is not
        ; a claim about the globals table. It was already reachable before the lib
        ; (`initialize_checker`'s merge_global_symbol), and exactly ONE unit
        ; witnessed it — walkcheck's standing red on `fixtures_globals_conflicts`.
        ; ★★★Slice 110 named that mechanism exactly and still filed it as a red to
        ; REPRODUCE at the parent tree, which is the right test for *whose commit*
        ; and no test at all for *whose LAYER*: the stop is the checker's and the
        ; empty list is the INSTRUMENT's. The lib turned one witness into nine, and
        ; that is the only reason it was diagnosed.
        if this.stop_walking()
            return

        let reparsed (AstNode.flags_of(elem) & NodeFlagsReparsed) <> 0
        let k AstNode.kind_of(elem)

        ; elem.Flags&Reparsed == 0 || elem.Kind == As || elem.Kind == Satisfies ||
        ;   ((parent.Kind == Satisfies || parent.Kind == As) && elem == parent.Expression())
        var descend false
        if reparsed = false
            set descend: true
        if k = KindAsExpression
            set descend: true
        if k = KindSatisfiesExpression
            set descend: true
        if descend = false
        {
            let p AstNode.parent_node_of(elem)
            if p <> null
            {
                let pk AstNode.kind_of(p)
                var under_assertion false
                if pk = KindSatisfiesExpression
                    set under_assertion: true
                if pk = KindAsExpression
                    set under_assertion: true
                if under_assertion
                {
                    if AstNode.expression_of(p) = elem
                        set descend: true
                }
            }
        }
        if descend = false
            return

        ; elem.Flags&Reparsed == 0 || parent.Kind == As || parent.Kind == Satisfies
        var ask false
        if reparsed = false
            set ask: true
        if ask = false
        {
            let p AstNode.parent_node_of(elem)
            if p <> null
            {
                let pk AstNode.kind_of(p)
                if pk = KindAsExpression
                    set ask: true
                if pk = KindSatisfiesExpression
                    set ask: true
            }
        }
        if ask
            this.visit(elem)
        if this.stop_walking()
            return

        var i 0
        let count AstNode.child_count(elem)
        while i < count
        {
            this.walk(AstNode.child_at(elem, i))
            if this.stop_walking()
                return
            set i: i + 1
        }
    }

    ; ★ The walk's three `c.IsUnported()` exits, as ONE question — and the answer
    ; is NO in walk-only mode. See the note at the head of `walk`.
    function stop_walking(this) returns bool
    {
        if walk_only
            return false
        c.is_unported()
    }

    ; The oracle's per-node body: skip the unit itself and the excluded shapes, ask
    ; for the type, print one line.
    procedure visit(this, node: ref[AstNode])
    {
        if node = file
            return
        if this.skip_for_type(node)
            return
        ; The oracle's own exclusion; see the header.
        if TypeDump.is_nameless_type_only_import_clause(node)
            return

        ; The instrument stops here: the node IS in the list, and what it would be
        ; asked is not what the instrument measures.
        if walk_only
        {
            out.append("W ")
            out.append(AstNode.kind_of(node))
            out.append(" " as char)
            out.append(AstNode.pos_of(node))
            out.append(" " as char)
            out.append(AstNode.end_of(node))
            out.append("\n" as char)
            return
        }

        var t: ref[Type]? null

        ; The reference's base-class workaround, verbatim in intent: ask the
        ; ExpressionWithTypeArguments so that `class D extends C` reports `C` and
        ; not `typeof C`.
        let p AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
        if AstNode.is_expression_with_type_arguments_in_class_extends_clause(p)
        {
            set t: c.get_type_of_node(p)
            if c.is_unported()
                return
        }

        var reask false
        if t = null
            set reask: true
        if Checker.is_type_any(t)
            set reask: true
        if reask
        {
            set t: c.get_type_of_node(node)
            if c.is_unported()
                return
        }

        ; The reference SKIPS a nil type. Ours cannot be nil without a report — a
        ; ported arm of getTypeOfNode always answers a type — so reaching here with
        ; nil would be a defect in this file rather than a node with no type.
        if t = null
            return

        ; ★★★ THE NAME IS BUILT INTO A SCRATCH BUILDER AND NOT INTO `out`, AND
        ; THE FUNCTION'S CONTRACT IS WHY: type_to_string composes since slice 71,
        ; so a type it cannot name may already have had part of a name written
        ; before it answers false. A scratch builder makes that partial write
        ; unobservable; writing into the real sink would leave a T line's tail in
        ; the dump of a unit that then reports unported.
        let name StringBuilder^host()
        let named c.type_to_string(t, &name)
        if c.is_unported()
            return
        if named = false
            return

        out.append("T ")
        out.append(AstNode.kind_of(node))
        out.append(" " as char)
        out.append(AstNode.pos_of(node))
        out.append(" " as char)
        out.append(AstNode.end_of(node))
        out.append(" " as char)
        ; ★★ The oracle ESCAPES the type string — backslash, newline, carriage
        ; return, tab — because a type name is not a token and the dump's last
        ; field may contain spaces but not a line break. Through slice 70 every
        ; name was one of fourteen static KEYWORDS and there was nothing to
        ; escape; slice 71's string literal changed that, and the two escapes
        ; COMPOSE rather than overlap: escapeStringWorker turns a raw backslash in
        ; the VALUE into `\\` and this turns each of those into `\\\\`, which is
        ; what the oracle's own two passes produce.
        TypeDump.append_escaped(out, name.to_string())
        out.append("\n" as char)
    }

    ; tests/oracle/types.go's `escape`, in its own four-replacement order — which
    ; is an order that matters: the backslash pass must run FIRST, or the
    ; backslashes the other three introduce would be doubled by it.
    procedure append_escaped(out: ref[StringBuilder], s: String)
    {
        let n s.get_length() as int
        let p s.as_slice()
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            let ch p[i] as int
            if ch = 0x5C
                out.append("\\\\")
            else
            {
                if ch = 0x0A
                    out.append("\\n")
                else
                {
                    if ch = 0x0D
                        out.append("\\r")
                    else
                    {
                        if ch = 0x09
                            out.append("\\t")
                        else
                            out.append(p[i])
                    }
                }
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
    }

    ; skipForType, transcribed from writeTypeOrSymbol's non-symbol half.
    function skip_for_type(this, node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.is_part_of_type_node(node)
            return true

        let k AstNode.kind_of(node)
        ; An `as`/`satisfies` whose TYPE is reparsed is a JSDoc-derived assertion,
        ; i.e. syntax that is not in the source.
        var assertion false
        if k = KindAsExpression
            set assertion: true
        if k = KindSatisfiesExpression
            set assertion: true
        if assertion
        {
            let ty AstNode.type_of(node)
            if ty <> null
            {
                if (AstNode.flags_of(ty) & NodeFlagsReparsed) <> 0
                    return true
            }
        }

        ; An IDENTIFIER that declares nothing into the VALUE space is not asked
        ; about — with the type-alias NAME as the exception, which is the one place
        ; a type declaration's name does have a type worth printing.
        if k = KindIdentifier
        {
            let p AstNode.parent_node_of(node)
            if (c.get_meaning_from_declaration(p) & SemanticMeaningValue) = 0
            {
                var is_alias_name false
                if AstNode.is_type_or_js_type_alias_declaration(p)
                {
                    if AstNode.name_of(p) = node
                        set is_alias_name: true
                }
                if is_alias_name = false
                    return true
            }
        }

        k = KindOmittedExpression
    }

    ; ★★★ THE ONE EXCLUSION THAT IS OURS, mirrored from tests/oracle/types.go
    ; because a node the ORACLE will not ask about must not appear on this side
    ; either. `import type { A } from './x'` — a type-only import clause with named
    ; bindings and no name — is an ast.IsTypeDeclaration whose
    ; getSymbolOfDeclaration answers nil (the SPECIFIERS carry the symbols), and
    ; getDeclaredTypeOfSymbol then dereferences it: SIGSEGV, from one line of valid
    ; TypeScript through a public API. The reference's own type baseline never meets
    ; it because the corpus cases carrying that syntax declare
    ; `@noTypesAndSymbols: true`.
    ;
    ; ★ It is written to the SHAPE and not to `GetSymbolAtLocation() == nil`, which
    ; is a different question and would drop a nameless `export default class {}`'s
    ; type as well. The three neighbouring spellings are all fine and each says why
    ; this one is not: a value import is no type declaration, a default type import
    ; has a name and therefore a symbol, and the nameless default class has one too
    ; ("default"). Bounded by measurement on the oracle's side: 0 failures over
    ; 2 867 files.
    function is_nameless_type_only_import_clause(node: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if AstNode.kind_of(node) <> KindImportClause
            return false
        if AstNode.is_type_only_of(node) = false
            return false
        AstNode.name_of(node) = null
    }
}
